/**
\page testing_tools Testing and development tools

[ \ref eapol_test "eapol_test" |
\ref preauth_test "preauth_test" |
\ref driver_test "driver_test" |
\ref unit_tests "Unit tests" |
\ref wpa_trace "Tracing code" ]

wpa_supplicant source tree includes number of testing and development
tools that make it easier to test the programs without having to setup
a full test setup with wireless cards. In addition, these tools can be
used to implement automatic tests suites.

\section eapol_test eapol_test - EAP peer and RADIUS client testing

eapol_test is a program that links together the same EAP peer
implementation that wpa_supplicant is using and the RADIUS
authentication client code from hostapd. In addition, it has minimal
glue code to combine these two components in similar ways to IEEE
802.1X/EAPOL Authenticator state machines. In other words, it
integrates IEEE 802.1X Authenticator (normally, an access point) and
IEEE 802.1X Supplicant (normally, a wireless client) together to
generate a single program that can be used to test EAP methods without
having to setup an access point and a wireless client.

The main uses for eapol_test are in interoperability testing of EAP
methods against RADIUS servers and in development testing for new EAP
methods. It can be easily used to automate EAP testing for
interoperability and regression since the program can be run from
shell scripts without require additional test components apart from a
RADIUS server. For example, the automated EAP tests described in
eap_testing.txt are implemented with eapol_test. Similarly, eapol_test
could be used to implement an automated regression test suite for a
RADIUS authentication server.

eapol_test uses the same build time configuration file, .config, as
wpa_supplicant. This file is used to select which EAP methods are
included in eapol_test. This program is not built with the default
Makefile target, so a separate make command needs to be used to
compile the tool:

\verbatim
make eapol_test
\endverbatim

The resulting eapol_test binary has following command like options:

\verbatim
usage:
eapol_test [-nWS] -c<conf> [-a<AS IP>] [-p<AS port>] [-s<AS secret>] \
           [-r<count>] [-t<timeout>] [-C<Connect-Info>] \
           [-M<client MAC address>]
eapol_test scard
eapol_test sim <PIN> <num triplets> [debug]

options:
  -c<conf> = configuration file
  -a<AS IP> = IP address of the authentication server, default 127.0.0.1
  -p<AS port> = UDP port of the authentication server, default 1812
  -s<AS secret> = shared secret with the authentication server, default 'radius'
  -r<count> = number of re-authentications
  -W = wait for a control interface monitor before starting
  -S = save configuration after authentiation
  -n = no MPPE keys expected
  -t<timeout> = sets timeout in seconds (default: 30 s)
  -C<Connect-Info> = RADIUS Connect-Info (default: CONNECT 11Mbps 802.11b)
  -M<client MAC address> = Set own MAC address (Calling-Station-Id,
                           default: 02:00:00:00:00:01)
\endverbatim


As an example,
\verbatim
eapol_test -ctest.conf -a127.0.0.1 -p1812 -ssecret -r1
\endverbatim
tries to complete EAP authentication based on the network
configuration from test.conf against the RADIUS server running on the
local host. A re-authentication is triggered to test fast
re-authentication. The configuration file uses the same format for
network blocks as wpa_supplicant.


\section preauth_test preauth_test - WPA2 pre-authentication and EAP peer testing

preauth_test is similar to eapol_test in the sense that in combines
EAP peer implementation with something else, in this case, with WPA2
pre-authentication. This tool can be used to test pre-authentication
based on the code that wpa_supplicant is using. As such, it tests
both the wpa_supplicant implementation and the functionality of an
access point.

preauth_test is built with:

\verbatim
make preauth_test
\endverbatim

and it uses following command line arguments:

\verbatim
usage: preauth_test <conf> <target MAC address> <ifname>
\endverbatim

For example,
\verbatim
preauth_test test.conf 02:11:22:33:44:55 eth0
\endverbatim
would use network configuration from test.conf to try to complete
pre-authentication with AP using BSSID 02:11:22:33:44:55. The
pre-authentication packets would be sent using the eth0 interface.


\section driver_test driver_test - user space test driver for hostapd and wpa_supplicant

%wpa_supplicant and hostapd use the \ref driver_wrapper "driver interface
API" to talk to the WLAN driver. driver_test.c implements that API without
any kernel driver, cfg80211/mac80211, or WLAN hardware: management frames
and EAPOL frames are exchanged between hostapd (driver=test) and
%wpa_supplicant (-Dtest) instances through a local UNIX domain datagram
socket (or a loopback UDP socket). Since the same driver API is used as with
real drivers, the complete protocol implementation (IEEE 802.11
authentication/association state machines in hostapd, SME in
%wpa_supplicant, RSN/EAPOL/EAP/RADIUS, FT, SAE, OWE, FILS, PMF, MLO) is
exercised; only the driver_*.c layer itself is replaced by the test
transport.

The test driver is enabled with the following line in .config of both
hostapd and %wpa_supplicant:

\verbatim
CONFIG_DRIVER_TEST=y
\endverbatim

\subsection driver_test_design Design

hostapd sees the test driver as a mac80211-like driver with AP MLME in
hostapd: received management frames are delivered with EVENT_RX_MGMT,
hostapd transmits frames with send_mlme() and receives asynchronous
EVENT_TX_STATUS, stations are tracked with sta_add()/sta_remove(),
keys with set_key(), and EAPOL frames use hapd_send_eapol() and
EVENT_EAPOL_RX. %wpa_supplicant sees a driver with WPA_DRIVER_FLAGS_SME:
scan2() sends Probe Request frames and collects Probe Response frames
into scan results, authenticate() and associate() build and send the
corresponding management frames, and EVENT_AUTH/EVENT_ASSOC/
EVENT_ASSOC_REJECT/EVENT_DEAUTH/EVENT_DISASSOC/EVENT_AUTH_TIMED_OUT/
EVENT_ASSOC_TIMED_OUT are generated from the received frames and
deterministic timers. EAPOL frames use the control port operations
(WPA_DRIVER_FLAGS_CONTROL_PORT and WPA_DRIVER_FLAGS2_CONTROL_PORT_RX).

Because there is no kernel network device, the driver reports
WPA_DRIVER_FLAGS2_NO_NETDEV. %wpa_supplicant and hostapd then skip the
l2_packet sockets that would normally be opened on the interface name
(EAPOL, TDLS, FT RRB, ETH_P_OUI); FT RRB messages are still delivered
between BSSes within the same hostapd process.

Every datagram uses a 16-octet versioned header (see
src/drivers/driver_test.h): magic "HTD1", version, message type
(management frame, EAPOL frame, or liveness probe), flags (protected
frame, no-ack), MLD link ID, the sender's operating frequency, the
payload length, and a sequence counter. Datagrams with an invalid header
or a payload length that does not match the datagram length are dropped
and counted; the counters are available with the STATUS-DRIVER control
interface command. Peer tables, scan result storage, template copies and
element buffers are bounded. Sockets are non-blocking so that a slow or
malicious peer cannot stall hostapd or %wpa_supplicant.

Key material is not stored by the test driver and frames are never
encrypted. The driver does, however, track which keys have been
configured and emulates the protected/unprotected distinction of
robust management frames (deauthentication, disassociation, robust
Action frames): frames transmitted while a pairwise key exists are marked
protected, unprotected robust frames from a peer with which management
frame protection has been negotiated are dropped, and frames marked
protected without a matching key are dropped. This allows PMF and SA
Query paths to be exercised.

\subsection driver_test_params Driver parameters

hostapd: driver_params=&lt;params&gt; in the configuration file;
%wpa_supplicant: driver_param=&lt;params&gt; in the configuration file or
-p &lt;params&gt; on the command line. Parameters are space separated.

- test_socket=&lt;path&gt;: hostapd binds a UNIX datagram socket at path;
  %wpa_supplicant sends to that path and binds STA-&lt;ifname&gt; in the
  same directory.
- test_dir=&lt;dir&gt;: hostapd binds &lt;dir&gt;/AP-&lt;ifname&gt;;
  %wpa_supplicant binds &lt;dir&gt;/STA-&lt;ifname&gt; and discovers every
  AP-* socket in the directory when scanning (multiple APs).
- test_udp=&lt;port&gt;: hostapd binds 127.0.0.1:port; %wpa_supplicant
  sends to 127.0.0.1:port. UDP and UNIX transports have identical
  semantics (local, unreliable datagrams); UNIX sockets additionally
  allow deterministic detection of a vanished peer.
- scan_time=&lt;ms&gt; (default 200), auth_timeout=&lt;ms&gt; (2000),
  assoc_timeout=&lt;ms&gt; (2000), keepalive=&lt;ms&gt; (1000): timers for
  scan completion, authentication/association timeouts, and the liveness
  probe used to detect a vanished AP.
- phy=ht,vht,he,eht|all|none: which synthetic HT/VHT/HE/EHT capability
  elements the station includes when the AP advertises the corresponding
  operation elements (default all).
- mlo=0|1: report WPA_DRIVER_FLAGS2_MLO (default 1 when CONFIG_IEEE80211BE
  is enabled).

The interface names (hostapd interface=, %wpa_supplicant -i) can be chosen
freely; they do not need to exist. The MAC address is derived
deterministically from the interface name unless hostapd bssid= is set.

\subsection driver_test_example Example: WPA2-PSK

hostapd configuration (psk-test.conf):

\verbatim
driver=test
interface=ap1
driver_params=test_socket=/tmp/test/ap1
ctrl_interface=/tmp/test/hostapd-ctrl
ssid=jkm-test-psk
hw_mode=g
channel=1
ieee80211n=1
wpa=2
wpa_key_mgmt=WPA-PSK
rsn_pairwise=CCMP
wpa_passphrase=12345678
\endverbatim

%wpa_supplicant configuration (psk-test.conf):

\verbatim
ctrl_interface=/tmp/test/wpas-ctrl
driver_param=test_socket=/tmp/test/ap1
network={
    ssid="jkm-test-psk"
    key_mgmt=WPA-PSK
    psk="12345678"
}
\endverbatim

\verbatim
mkdir -p /tmp/test
hostapd psk-test.conf &
wpa_supplicant -Dtest -ista1 -c psk-test.conf
\endverbatim

%wpa_supplicant prints:

\verbatim
sta1: SME: Trying to authenticate with 02:83:16:8a:24:7e (SSID='jkm-test-psk' freq=2412 MHz)
sta1: Trying to associate with 02:83:16:8a:24:7e (SSID='jkm-test-psk' freq=2412 MHz)
sta1: Associated with 02:83:16:8a:24:7e
sta1: WPA: Key negotiation completed with 02:83:16:8a:24:7e [PTK=CCMP GTK=CCMP]
sta1: CTRL-EVENT-CONNECTED - Connection to 02:83:16:8a:24:7e completed [id=0 id_str=]
\endverbatim

Multiple stations can be started as separate processes or with the -N
option (e.g., -ista1 -N -Dtest -ista2). Multiple BSSes (bss= blocks) and
AP MLD links (mld_ap=1 with the same interface= name in separate
configuration files) share the radio socket of the first BSS.

\subsection driver_test_limits Limitations

The test driver exercises protocol and state machine behavior only. It does
not emulate radio scheduling, channel widths, PHY rate negotiation,
puncturing, spatial streams, airtime, beacon transmission, channel
switching countdowns, DFS/CAC, regulatory enforcement, EML operating
modes, TID-to-link mapping negotiation, over-the-air behavior, or
encryption. Frequencies reported to the higher layers are the configured
values; scan results use a constant synthetic signal level. There is no
data path (no Data frames, DHCP, or IP connectivity), no TDLS, no mesh or
IBSS, no AP mode in %wpa_supplicant, and no P2P group operation. Reported
hardware capabilities (channels, HT/VHT/HE/EHT capabilities) are static
synthetic values chosen to exercise configuration and element paths.

\section unit_tests Unit tests

Number of the components (.c files) used in wpa_supplicant define
their own unit tests for automated validation of the basic
functionality. Most of the tests for cryptographic algorithms are
using standard test vectors to validate functionality. These tests can
be useful especially when verifying port to a new CPU target.

The test programs are collected in the tests subdirectory. All
automated unit tests can be run with

\verbatim
make run-tests
\endverbatim

This make target builds and runs each test and terminates with zero
exit code if all tests were completed successfully.


\section wpa_trace Tracing code for developer debuggin

wpa_supplicant and hostapd can be built with tracing code that will
track and analyze memory allocations and other resource registrations
and certain API uses. If incorrect use is detected, a backtrace of the
call location (and/or allocation location) is shown. This can also be
used to detect certain categories of memory leaks and report them
automatically when the program is terminated. The report will also
include information about forgotten eloop events.

The trace code can be enabled with CONFIG_WPA_TRACE=y build
option. More verbose backtrace information can be generated if libbfd
is available and the binaries are not stripped of symbol
information. This is enabled with CONFIG_WPA_TRACE_BFD=y.

For example, a memory leak (forgotten os_free() call) would show up
like this when the program is terminated:

\verbatim
MEMLEAK[0x82d200]: len 128
WPA_TRACE: memleak - START
[0]: ./wpa_supplicant(os_malloc+0x59) [0x41a5e9]
     os_malloc() ../src/utils/os_unix.c:359
[1]: ./wpa_supplicant(os_zalloc+0x16) [0x41a676]
     os_zalloc() ../src/utils/os_unix.c:418
[2]: ./wpa_supplicant(wpa_supplicant_init+0x38) [0x48b508]
     wpa_supplicant_init() wpa_supplicant.c:2315
[3]: ./wpa_supplicant(main+0x2f3) [0x491073]
     main() main.c:252
WPA_TRACE: memleak - END
MEMLEAK: total 128 bytes
\endverbatim

Another type of error that can be detected is freeing of memory area
that was registered for some use and is still be referenced:

\verbatim
WPA_TRACE: Freeing referenced memory - START
[2]: ./wpa_supplicant(os_free+0x5c) [0x41a53c]
     os_free() ../src/utils/os_unix.c:411
[3]: ./wpa_supplicant(wpa_supplicant_remove_iface+0x30) [0x48b380]
     wpa_supplicant_remove_iface() wpa_supplicant.c:2259
[4]: ./wpa_supplicant(wpa_supplicant_deinit+0x20) [0x48b3e0]
     wpa_supplicant_deinit() wpa_supplicant.c:2430
[5]: ./wpa_supplicant(main+0x357) [0x4910d7]
     main() main.c:276
WPA_TRACE: Freeing referenced memory - END
WPA_TRACE: Reference registration - START
[1]: ./wpa_supplicant [0x41c040]
     eloop_trace_sock_add_ref() ../src/utils/eloop.c:94
[2]: ./wpa_supplicant(wpa_supplicant_ctrl_iface_deinit+0x17) [0x473247]
     wpa_supplicant_ctrl_iface_deinit() ctrl_iface_unix.c:436
[3]: ./wpa_supplicant [0x48b21c]
     wpa_supplicant_cleanup() wpa_supplicant.c:378
     wpa_supplicant_deinit_iface() wpa_supplicant.c:2155
[4]: ./wpa_supplicant(wpa_supplicant_remove_iface+0x30) [0x48b380]
     wpa_supplicant_remove_iface() wpa_supplicant.c:2259
[5]: ./wpa_supplicant(wpa_supplicant_deinit+0x20) [0x48b3e0]
     wpa_supplicant_deinit() wpa_supplicant.c:2430
[6]: ./wpa_supplicant(main+0x357) [0x4910d7]
     main() main.c:276
WPA_TRACE: Reference registration - END
Aborted
\endverbatim

This type of error results in showing backtraces for both the location
where the incorrect freeing happened and the location where the memory
area was marked referenced.

*/
